Your own periodic jobs, one per line, started by your session at login.

    NAME  TIMESPEC...  --  COMMAND...

The shape is /etc/kdos/timers.d's, and TIMESPEC is `snooze`'s own options —
`-H 9 -M 0` for nine in the morning, `-d /2` for every second day. `snooze(1)`
is the whole reference; nothing is invented here. There is no `--help`.

`-s SLACK` IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. A snooze that has just started begins
looking one second from now, so a slot already past is simply the same slot
next time round, whatever the slack says. `-t FILE` is what changes that: it
starts looking from that file's modification time, and the slack then covers
the gap. `-s 4h -t ~/.config/kdos/timers.d/mine.timer` means "if the machine
was off at the slot, run it once when I log in, up to four hours late".

THE COMMAND IS AN ARGUMENT VECTOR. No pipe, no redirection, no `&&`: a program
that writes a file takes a flag naming it. That is what lets this file be
parsed instead of sourced, and it is why a line here cannot run something you
did not write.

EVERY SLOT WHILE THE LOOP LIVES. `snooze` waits for its slot, runs the command
once and exits, so the session runs each row in a loop that starts it again: a
job here fires at every occurrence of its slot, not once per login. The loops
are the session's children, not a root service, and their process ids are in
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kdos/timers.pid. Nothing stops them when you log out; the
next login on the same boot stops the previous set before starting its own,
and a reboot ends them. Use /etc/kdos/timers.d for a job that must run whether
or not anybody has logged in since boot.

A COMMAND HERE CANNOT TAKE AN ARGUMENT WITH A SPACE IN IT. The row is split
into words and never quoted, so `-- kdos notify "Good morning"` reaches the
command as `"Good` and `morning"`. A program that needs a sentence reads it
from a file of its own; `kdos remind` writes its text as this file's first
comment line for exactly that reason.

An example, commented out because a table with a job in it is a decision:

  # weather  -H 7 -M 30 -s 2h  --  kdos notify --time
